# The Windows CI health job of 2026-11-13: what it fixed, broken again one
# rule at a time (docs/steps/11/answered.md, "Windows CI health").

[break] test-server-port-closes-with-its-thread
rule: The scripted server's port stays open until its accepting thread has ended, so that the connection that wakes the thread is never refused (a refusal takes two seconds on Windows).
file: crates/cortex-testkit/src/server.rs
in: SseServer::start
find: listener: Some(kept)
replace: listener: None
caught-by:
    server::tests::the_port_stays_open_until_the_accepting_thread_has_ended

# The two below break the rule that decides what a Windows Job Object's list
# of members means. The function is built on every system so that these run
# anywhere. The code that asks the job is Windows-only: the test that puts
# the question the instant a command has exited
# (`tree::sys_a_command_that_left_nothing_is_never_counted_itself`) is what
# holds it, on a Windows runner.

[break] the-command-is-counted-among-what-it-left
rule: On Windows a job lists a command for a moment after its exit; the command is never one of the processes it left running.
file: crates/cortex-sandbox/src/tree/mod.rs
in: others_among
find: **member != command && !has_exited(**member)
replace: !has_exited(**member)
caught-by:
    tree::tests::the_command_itself_is_never_one_of_the_processes_it_left

[break] an-exited-member-is-counted-as-left-running
rule: A member of the job that has already exited is not a process the command left running.
file: crates/cortex-sandbox/src/tree/mod.rs
in: others_among
find: **member != command && !has_exited(**member)
replace: **member != command
caught-by:
    tree::tests::a_member_that_has_exited_is_not_left_running
